program            = 004_case_ranges
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC case ranges (case LOW ... HIGH:) including negative, character, degenerate single-value and fallthrough ranges
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them, which is measured rather than asserted.
The canonical record of this deviation is the matching paragraph of impl_defined_notes
below, which is the one field
this suite reads a gate reason from; it is restated here so that the whole argument for
this program's fitness as test material can be read in one place.

UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every value is a small int; the largest computation is 1 + 10, far
from INT_MIN and INT_MAX, so there is no signed overflow. No shift operator is used. No
pointer, array or aggregate is used, so there is no aliasing violation and no
one-past-end pointer. Every object is initialized before it is read. No object is
modified twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at most one
side-effecting argument. Nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of
unrelated objects. Every switch has a default label, so control flow is total for every
possible operand value, and the case ranges within any one switch never overlap, so no
label is ambiguous. The sweep loop runs from -25 to 35 inclusive and always terminates.

Every switch operand is int, never plain char. The character constants used as range
bounds have type int in C. This is deliberate: char was measured as signed on x86_64 and
i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, so a plain-char operand could change which
range matches on different targets and manufacture a divergence caused by the test rather
than by the compiler.

The deliberate fallthrough between two case ranges carries a /* fall through */ comment,
which GCC recognizes to suppress -Wimplicit-fallthrough; -Wextra enables that warning and
-Werror makes it fatal, so the comment is required for the gate to pass.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them, which is measured rather than asserted.
A gate deviation is not a divergence: the gate is a property of the TEST MATERIAL, it is driven by the reference
compiler only, and it renders no verdict about bcc.

Nothing implementation-defined is observed, so no target restriction is needed and all
four targets are compared. Only int values are printed, so the measured width differences
cannot be reached: sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64. Plain char is never used as a switch operand or a printed value, so the
measured char-signedness difference cannot be reached. No address or pointer value is
printed.

Case range syntax requires a space on both sides of the ellipsis, which is measured
rather than assumed: writing case 1...5: without spaces produces "error: too many decimal
points in number", because the lexer reads 1. followed by ..5 as a malformed floating
constant. Every range in the program keeps the spaces.

The runtime probes use volatile int operands so the optimizer cannot fold the switch away
and must emit a genuine lowering. Dense and sparse label sets select different lowering
strategies, a jump table versus a comparison chain, and only the runtime variant
exercises them.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.

THIS PROGRAM CARRIES THE EXPECTED-DIVERGENCE MARKER XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001, and the marker
block below is the machine-readable half of it. What follows is why the marker exists, what it
does and does not excuse, and what it would take to retire it.

WHY CASE RANGES ARE THE ONE CONSTRUCT IN THE MANDATED EXTENSION LIST THAT NEEDS A MARKER. The
mandated list of GCC extensions is statement expressions, typeof, computed gotos and case ranges.
The first three appear in the repository's documented extension inventory; case ranges appear in
none of them. docs/project-guide.md line 206 is that inventory: its Compliance and Quality Review
row for the C11 frontend records a passing verdict and enumerates exactly what is parsed --
__attribute__, statement expressions, typeof, computed goto, inline assembly -- and case ranges are
not among them. docs/technical-specifications.md lines 13, 107 and 506 enumerate the same set in
three further places, with the same absence.

WHAT THAT BASIS DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH, STATED PLAINLY SO THE MARKER IS NOT READ AS MORE THAN
IT IS. The basis is an OMISSION, and an omission is weaker than a statement: an inventory that does
not name a feature is consistent with the feature working and the inventory being incomplete, with
the feature not working, and with nobody having considered the question. The marker is therefore not
a claim that bcc must reject case ranges. It is a claim that IF bcc rejects them, the rejection is
traceable to a documented gap rather than being an unexplained divergence -- which is exactly the
distinction requirement 5 draws, and exactly the ambiguity the plan flagged for a maintainer to
resolve. The register records the same qualification, and the citation is machine-resolved against
line 206 of the cited document on every run, so a reader can turn to the inventory and judge it.

WHAT STANDS BEHIND EACH HALF OF THE OBSERVATION, BECAUSE THE TWO HALVES ARE NOT EQUALLY EVIDENCED.
The reference half is measured: gcc 13.4.0 accepts this program and prints the eighteen-line golden
record below, on all four targets at all three optimization levels. The compiler-under-test half
carries no independent capture, and expected_divergence.evidence is left unwritten rather than filled
in from something weaker. This checkout carries the corpus and no bcc, and the only compiler under
test it can offer forwards every invocation to the pinned reference driver for the requested target --
so that compiler's agreement with the reference compiler is a measurement of the environment, in which
one toolchain is compared with itself, and it is evidence about neither bcc nor case ranges. A run of
that shape has been performed here, and it is recorded for exactly what it is: non-independent
environment evidence, which settles nothing in either direction.

WHY THE MARKER IS PRESENT ANYWAY, WHICH IS THE PART THAT KEEPS BEING GOT WRONG. The project
specification is frozen, and it fixes this marker for this program by identifier, class, scope and
basis. Carrying it is therefore a requirement of the specification rather than an inference this
record may withdraw -- and the same reasoning already governs the basis, because the record format
admits an omission-based citation precisely on the ground that refusing it would refuse a marker the
specification mandates, which makes the format wrong rather than the record. What the specification
does not do is suspend the safeguard, and the safeguard is what keeps carrying this marker honest:
where the compiler under test accepts the construct, the arm this marker scopes agrees, the verdict is
XPASS and the run FAILS, naming the marker and both places it lives. Nothing is quietly excused. An
unconfirmed marker is a loud, run-failing state rather than a silent pass, and
BCC_CONFORMANCE_ALLOW_XPASS is the transition window the specification provides for precisely that.

WHAT THE MARKER DOES NOT CHANGE. Constraint C3 forbids excluding a feature because it is difficult,
and nothing here is excluded: the program is written, compiled and executed on all four targets at all
three optimization levels, exactly like every other program in the corpus, and all three oracles stay
enabled -- twelve bcc cells, on the same footing as every other program. A marker changes how a
divergence is CLASSIFIED, never whether the feature is EXERCISED. If bcc accepts the syntax and
computes a wrong result, that is a stdout_mismatch, which this marker's class does not cover, and it is
reported as the FINDING it is -- delivered with a verbatim reproducer, the captured output of each
compiler and each backend, an environment fingerprint and the exact reproduction commands.

THE MARKER'S SCOPE IS ORACLE (a) ALONE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Oracle (a) is where "the reference
compiler accepted this program and the compiler under test did not" is a statement about the two
compilers, so it is the arm where a refusal is genuinely an expected divergence. Oracles (b) and (c)
do not observe a divergence when a build is refused -- they lose their subject. Widening the scope to
name them would have them claim a comparison they never made; leaving them to be classified
independently would file the same root event as two further findings. Neither is what happens: the
classifier settles the refusal once on oracle (a) against this marker, and reports the arms the same
root event BLOCKED as dependent blocked outcomes that name this marker and this arm, state that no
comparison was attempted, and file nothing of their own. One root event, one authority, every arm
visible.

RETIREMENT TAKES EVIDENCE, NOT THE ABSENCE OF IT, AND THIS RECORD IS EXPLICIT ABOUT IT BECAUSE THE
MARKER HAS BEEN MINTED AND WITHDRAWN SEVERAL TIMES ALREADY. The observation that retires this marker
is a real, independent bcc accepting this program and agreeing with the reference compiler -- the
actual compiler under test, not a stand-in forwarding to the reference toolchain, because a toolchain
compared with itself cannot answer the question the marker asks. With that observation in hand,
retirement is one edit in each of two places: delete the marker block from this record, and delete the
summary row and the structured entry from tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md. Should the
repository instead come to STATE the limitation in prose, cite that sentence and quote it in the
optional expected_divergence.documented key: the audit resolves the quotation inside the cited range,
and the basis becomes an assertion rather than a silence.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
cr_bucket_neg=10
cr_bucket_b1=10
cr_bucket_b2=10
cr_bucket_b3=1
cr_bucket_b4=10
cr_bucket_default=20
cr_hex_digit=7
cr_hex_lower=14
cr_hex_upper=11
cr_hex_none=-1
cr_single_hit=42
cr_single_miss=0
cr_fall_low=11
cr_fall_mid=10
cr_fall_high=100
cr_fall_none=0
cr_runtime_bucket=2
cr_runtime_hex=12
END
expected_divergence.id       = XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001
expected_divergence.class    = compile_failure
expected_divergence.scope    = oracle_a; all targets; all opt levels
expected_divergence.basis    = docs/project-guide.md, line 206, the documented GCC extension inventory enumerates the parsed extensions and omits case ranges
expected_divergence.documented <<END
__attribute__, statement expressions, typeof, computed goto, inline assembly all parsed
END
expected_divergence.observed <<END
The divergence this marker covers is a refusal by the compiler under test to translate the case-range
label form: no artifact is produced, so oracle (a) has nothing to compare, while the reference compiler
accepts the same source and prints the eighteen-line golden record this file carries for it, beginning cr_bucket_neg=10
and ending cr_runtime_hex=12. The reference half of that pair is measured on this branch, with
gcc 13.4.0 on all four targets at all three optimization levels. The compiler-under-test half carries
no independent capture, which is why expected_divergence.evidence is left unwritten: the only compiler
under test this checkout can offer forwards every invocation to the pinned reference driver, so its
agreement measures the environment -- one toolchain compared with itself -- and speaks to neither bcc
nor case ranges. The marker is present because the frozen project specification fixes it for this
program by identifier, class, scope and basis; the register's section 3.1 XPASS safeguard is what keeps that honest,
and only a real independent bcc observation retires it.
END
